escription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xxxv, 316 p. ; 24 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"Is inequality inevitable? Are certain groups or people in every society more \"equal\" than others? Slavery, the caste system, serfdom, class distinctions, and the bureaucratic inequality of the post-industrial age—these are some of the major forms of inequality that have existed and, in some cases, still exist in all societies. To answer these questions, the authors of POWER AND EQUITY employ a historical, comparative perspective to reveal not only why each of these five systems emerged but also the reasons for their continuation, erosion, or disappearance. Uniting social science with social philosophy, this text provides the student with a basic introduction to the subject of stratification and the arguments surrounding the problems of power and equity in any society."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"William McCord, Arline McCord."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references and index."}],"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Social classes","History"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Equality"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Power (Social sciences)"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Elite (Social sciences)"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Caste"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Social Class"}],"classification":{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"HT607 .M3 1977"},"relatedItem":[{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Online version:","titleInfo":{"title":"Power and equity"},"name":{"namePart":"McCord, William Maxwell, 1930-"},"originInfo":{"publisher":"New York : Praeger, 1977"},"identifier":{"@type":"local","#text":"(OCoLC)567913784"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990007329700203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0275227502 :"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^^75036205^"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":